{"model":"kimi-k3","created_at":"2026-09-08T21:47:59.20928657Z","response":"{\"description\": \"A three-round pure-Python project that incrementally evolves sensor-event summarization utilities in one module.py: a per-event severity classifier feeding a window summarizer that aggregates label counts, peaks, trends, muted-sensor handling and report metrics, with a mid-course label retirement, a scope-limited threshold override, and a superseding all-muted status rule.\", \"lineage\": \"Invented independently now for this schedule; fit-on none. Not a reproduction of any benchmark, paper example, existing challenge or previous experiment; no universal novelty claimed.\", \"initial_source\": \"\\\"\\\"\\\"Sensor-event summarization helpers.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n\\n\\ndef classify_event(event):\\n    kind = event.get(\\\"kind\\\")\\n    value = event.get(\\\"value\\\")\\n    if kind == \\\"alarm\\\":\\n        if value is None:\\n            return \\\"notice\\\"\\n        if value \u003e= 90:\\n            return \\\"critical\\\"\\n        if value \u003e= 50:\\n            return \\\"warning\\\"\\n        return \\\"notice\\\"\\n    if kind == \\\"reading\\\":\\n        if value is None:\\n            return \\\"unknown\\\"\\n        if value \u003e= 75:\\n            return \\\"high\\\"\\n        if value \u003c= 25:\\n            return \\\"low\\\"\\n        return \\\"normal\\\"\\n    if kind == \\\"heartbeat\\\":\\n        return \\\"ok\\\"\\n    return \\\"unknown\\\"\\n\\n\\ndef trend_of(events):\\n    values = []\\n    for event in events:\\n        if event.get(\\\"kind\\\") == \\\"reading\\\" and event.get(\\\"value\\\") is not None:\\n            values.append(event.get(\\\"value\\\"))\\n    if len(values) \u003c 2:\\n        return \\\"flat\\\"\\n    delta = values[-1] - values[0]\\n    if delta \u003e= 5:\\n        return \\\"rising\\\"\\n    if delta \u003c= -5:\\n        return \\\"falling\\\"\\n    return \\\"flat\\\"\\n\\n\\ndef summarize_window(events):\\n    counts = {}\\n    peak = None\\n    total = 0\\n    for event in events:\\n        total += 1\\n        label = classify_event(event)\\n        counts[label] = counts.get(label, 0) + 1\\n        if event.get(\\\"kind\\\") == \\\"reading\\\":\\n            value = event.get(\\\"value\\\")\\n            if value is not None and (peak is None or value \u003e peak):\\n                peak = value\\n    status = \\\"steady\\\"\\n    if counts.get(\\\"critical\\\"):\\n        status = \\\"alert\\\"\\n    elif counts.get(\\\"warning\\\") or counts.get(\\\"high\\\"):\\n        status = \\\"watch\\\"\\n    return {\\\"total\\\": total, \\\"counts\\\": counts, \\\"peak\\\": peak, \\\"status\\\": status}\\n\", \"rounds\": [{\"source_texts\": [\"We use summarize_window(events) in module.py for the on-call digest. events is a list of dicts like {\\\"kind\\\": \\\"reading\\\", \\\"value\\\": 61.5, \\\"ts\\\": 12}. Keep the returned total, counts, peak and status working exactly as they do now, but add a \\\"trend\\\" key whose value is trend_of(events), so the digest shows the window trend next to the counts.\", \"Some sensors get silenced during maintenance: their events carry \\\"muted\\\": True. Those events still happened, so keep counting them in \\\"total\\\", but exclude muted events from \\\"counts\\\", \\\"peak\\\", the status decision, and the trend (pass only the unmuted events to trend_of). Unflagged events behave exactly as before; the new \\\"trend\\\" key stays.\", \"Also add \\\"seen_kinds\\\": a sorted list of the distinct \\\"kind\\\" strings among unmuted events ([] when there are none). Ops wants it to confirm which sensor families reported. This is independent of the muted rule and of every existing key; nothing about total, counts, peak, status or trend changes.\", \"Edge cases to lock in: an empty window returns total 0, counts {}, peak None, status \\\"steady\\\", trend \\\"flat\\\", seen_kinds []. Events missing \\\"value\\\" must not crash peak. A teammate floated dropping muted events from \\\"total\\\" as well - that was just a hallway idea, not the requirement; muted events stay in total.\"], \"target_symbol\": \"summarize_window\"}, {\"source_texts\": [\"Time to clean up classify_event(event) in module.py. The label \\\"notice\\\" is retired: no input may produce it anymore. Everywhere \\\"notice\\\" used to come out (low-value alarms and alarms without a value), return \\\"info\\\" instead. All other labels, kinds and thresholds stay as they are.\", \"Add a new kind: \\\"fault\\\" events carry \\\"severity\\\" (an int from 1 to 5). Classify severity \u003e= 4 as \\\"critical\\\", severity 3 as \\\"warning\\\", and severity 1 or 2 as \\\"info\\\". A fault whose severity is missing or not a number becomes \\\"info\\\". Existing kinds (alarm, reading, heartbeat) and the unknown-kind fallback are unchanged.\", \"Alarms can now carry their own trip point as \\\"limit\\\". When an alarm has a numeric \\\"limit\\\" greater than 0, use it instead of the fixed 90/50 bands: value \u003e= limit is \\\"critical\\\", value \u003e= limit - 20 is \\\"warning\\\", below that \\\"info\\\". Alarms without a usable limit keep the old 90/50 bands, with the new \\\"info\\\" wording instead of \\\"notice\\\".\", \"Scope check so nothing gets cancelled by accident: the \\\"limit\\\" override affects only the bands of alarm events that carry a valid one; the fixed 90/50 bands still apply whenever limit is absent, None, or not greater than 0, and reading, heartbeat and fault logic does not change. The ops deck claims every fault is \\\"critical\\\" - that is marketing copy, not the spec. Retired \\\"notice\\\" stays gone from every path.\"], \"target_symbol\": \"classify_event\"}, {\"source_texts\": [\"Final push on summarize_window(events) in module.py for the nightly report. Add \\\"info_share\\\": the fraction of unmuted events classified \\\"info\\\", computed as round(info_count / unmuted_count, 3), or 0.0 when there are no unmuted events. Everything already specified (total, counts, peak, status, trend, seen_kinds, muted handling) stays in place.\", \"Add \\\"worst\\\": the highest-priority label currently present among unmuted events, using the order critical \u003e warning \u003e high \u003e low \u003e info \u003e normal \u003e ok \u003e unknown, and None when no unmuted events exist. This reads the live counts, which the retired \\\"info\\\" replacement feeds; do not resurrect \\\"notice\\\" anywhere in the summary.\", \"One explicit status change: if at least one event arrived but every event is muted, status is now \\\"silent\\\" instead of \\\"steady\\\". This supersedes only that all-muted case; the alert / watch / steady escalation for windows with unmuted events, plus the info-share and worst keys, keep working exactly as specified earlier.\", \"Lock the boundaries: an empty window keeps status \\\"steady\\\" (it was suggested empty should also be \\\"silent\\\" - that idea is rejected), worst None, info_share 0.0, trend \\\"flat\\\", seen_kinds [], counts {}. A mixed window with both muted and unmuted events never reports \\\"silent\\\", and the retired label \\\"notice\\\" remains absent from every key.\"], \"target_symbol\": \"summarize_window\"}]}","thinking":"Let me plan this carefully. I need to author a project schedule for \"sensor-event summarization\" with:\n\n- Project ID \"staged-04\"\n- A module.py with pure Python functions, one positional argument each, synchronous, no imports, no decorators, no annotations, no nesting, no top-level computations. Optional docstring.\n- Three rounds. Each round replaces exactly one existing synchronous one-argument top-level function. At least one target must be revisited (so e.g., round 3 targets the same function as round 1).\n- Each round has 4 source texts (user messages), each nonempty, ≤640 UTF-8 bytes.\n- Requirements distributed naturally across messages, with some ordinary context or clearly quoted non-authoritative material.\n- Round 1 must retain nontrivial initially specified behavior.\n- By round 2, introduce an explicit replacement or retirement that still matters in round 3, including in the final target.\n- Cancellation scope must be clear.\n- Maintain earlier behavior while implementing new behavior each round.\n- The initial module must contain each target function.\n\nDesign the domain: sensor-event summarization. Let's create a module that processes a list of sensor events (dicts) and produces a summary. Functions:\n\n- `summarize_window(events)` — main summarizer (target of round 1, and revisited in round 3).\n- `classify_event(event)` — classify a single event (target of round 2).\n- Maybe helpers: `bucket_label(value)`, `severity_score(event)`, `merge_readings(readings)`, `format_summary(summary)`.\n\nConstraints: each function one positional argument, no nesting, no annotations, no imports, no default args? \"one positional argument\" — exactly one positional argument. Default values might be okay but to keep safe, exactly one parameter each, no defaults.\n\nNo top-level computations — so no module-level constants computed... \"top-level computations\" presumably means no code executed at top level beyond function defs and maybe constant assignments? To be safe, use only function definitions and a docstring. Actually constant assignments like `LIMIT = 10` might count as top-level computation... it's an assignment, not a computation. But safer to inline constants in functions. Let's inline.\n\nEvents format: each event is a dict with keys like `\"kind\"` (e.g., \"reading\", \"alarm\", \"heartbeat\"), `\"value\"` (number), `\"unit\"`, `\"ts\"` (timestamp), `\"tags\"` maybe. Keep pure dict/list/number/str processing.\n\nInitial behavior:\n\n`classify_event(event)`: returns a string severity label based on kind and value:\n- \"alarm\" kind → \"critical\" if value \u003e= 90, else \"warning\" if \u003e= 50, else \"notice\".\n- \"reading\" kind → \"high\" if value \u003e= threshold e.g. 75, \"low\" if \u003c= 25, else \"normal\".\n- \"heartbeat\" → \"ok\".\n- unknown kind → \"unknown\".\n\n`summarize_window(events)`: takes list of events, returns dict with counts per severity (using classify_event), total events, max value among reading events maybe, and a \"status\" field: \"alert\" if any critical, \"watch\" if any warning or high, else \"steady\". Hmm, but classify_event returns labels; summarize counts.\n\nHelpers: `severity_of(event)` could just call classify... Let's keep functions:\n\n- `classify_event(event)` → string label.\n- `summarize_window(events)` → dict: {\"total\": n, \"counts\": {...}, \"peak\": max value of reading events or None, \"status\": ...}.\n- `trend_of(events)` → maybe computes trend \"rising\"/\"falling\"/\"flat\" from reading values. Could be a third target? Only one target must be revisited; targets: R1 summarize_window, R2 classify_event, R3 summarize_window (revisited). That satisfies \"at least one target must be revisited\". Other functions exist to be preserved but not changed.\n\nAlso maybe a helper `bucket_count` etc. Keep module compact but substantive.\n\nInitial source, module docstring optional; include short docstring.\n\nLet's write initial module:\n\n```python\n\"\"\"Sensor-event summarization helpers.\"\"\"\n\ndef classify_event(event):\n    kind = event.get(\"kind\")\n    value = event.get(\"value\")\n    if kind == \"alarm\":\n        if value is None:\n            return \"notice\"\n        if value \u003e= 90:\n            return \"critical\"\n        if value \u003e= 50:\n            return \"warning\"\n        return \"notice\"\n    if kind == \"reading\":\n        if value is None:\n            return \"unknown\"\n        if value \u003e= 75:\n            return \"high\"\n        if value \u003c= 25:\n            return \"low\"\n        return \"normal\"\n    if kind == \"heartbeat\":\n        return \"ok\"\n    return \"unknown\"\n\ndef summarize_window(events):\n    counts = {}\n    peak = None\n    total = 0\n    for event in events:\n        total += 1\n        label = classify_event(event)\n        counts[label] = counts.get(label, 0) + 1\n        if event.get(\"kind\") == \"reading\":\n            value = event.get(\"value\")\n            if value is not None:\n                if peak is None or value \u003e peak:\n                    peak = value\n    status = \"steady\"\n    if counts.get(\"critical\"):\n        status = \"alert\"\n    elif counts.get(\"warning\") or counts.get(\"high\"):\n        status = \"watch\"\n    return {\"total\": total, \"counts\": counts, \"peak\": peak, \"status\": status}\n\ndef trend_of(events):\n    values = []\n    for event in events:\n        if event.get(\"kind\") == \"reading\":\n            value = event.get(\"value\")\n            if value is not None:\n                values.append(value)\n    if len(values) \u003c 2:\n        return \"flat\"\n    delta = values[-1] - values[0]\n    if delta \u003e= 5:\n        return \"rising\"\n    if delta \u003c= -5:\n        return \"falling\"\n    return \"flat\"\n```\n\nAll functions one argument, no imports, no nesting, no annotations. Good.\n\nNow the rounds.\n\nRound 1: target `summarize_window`. New behavior while retaining nontrivial initial behavior (counts, peak, status). Add:\n- m01: context — team uses summarize_window for control-room digest; explain events are dicts; ask to add a \"labels\" ordered list? Hmm, want interacting conditions. Let's design:\n  - Add \"alerts\" list: summaries of critical/warning events as \"kind:value\" strings? Something testable.\n  - Add handling for events flagged `\"skip\": True` — excluded from counts and peak.\n  - Add \"peak_by\" maybe? Keep compact.\n  - Retain status logic, counts, peak.\n\nLet's design round 1 changes across 4 messages:\n- m01: Context: operations team; summarize_window currently returns total/counts/peak/status; keep those exactly. New: include \"trend\" key from trend_of(events).\n- m02: Add \"muted\" handling: events with \"muted\": True must be excluded from counts and peak and total? Careful with interacting conditions. Say: muted events are still counted in total (they arrived) but excluded from counts and peak and status. Hmm, that's a nice interacting condition. But careful about clarity. Message: \"Some sensors are silenced; events carry 'muted': True. Keep them in 'total' (they still occurred) but exclude from 'counts', 'peak', 'trend', and status.\" Wait trend is computed by trend_of which we are not changing this round — summarize can't change trend_of's input... Actually summarize_window can filter the list before passing to trend_of: `trend_of([e for e in events if not e.get(\"muted\")])`. That's fine; it's within summarize_window. Good—this shows interacting logic.\n- m03: Add \"labels\" list: ordered labels of non-muted events? That duplicates counts. Alternative: add \"alarm_total\": sum of values for alarm events? Let's add \"watchdog\": hmm. Add \"hottest\": the value of the single highest-priority event? Keep simpler: add \"seen_kinds\": sorted list of distinct kinds among non-muted events. Sorted for determinism.\n- m04: Boundary: empty window and missing keys. Confirm empty list returns total 0, counts {}, peak None, status \"steady\", trend \"flat\", seen_kinds [status]... Also clarify: non-dict events? Maybe too much. Add: events whose value is missing shouldn't crash peak. Also clarify quoted non-authoritative material: e.g., \"A colleague suggested ignoring muted events entirely and dropping them from total too — that was just a hallway idea, not the requirement.\" That satisfies \"clearly quoted non-authoritative material.\"\n\nWait, requirement says \"include some ordinary context or clearly quoted non-authoritative material\" — across the schedule. Good.\n\nRound 1 retains: counts per label via classify_event, peak of reading values, status escalations, total. Nontrivial retained behavior: yes.\n\nRound 2: target `classify_event`. Introduce explicit replacement/retirement here that matters in round 3 including final target. Since round 3 revisits summarize_window (final target), round 2's retirement must matter there — e.g., the \"notice\" label is retired and replaced by \"info\", which changes summarize_window's counts keys and status logic (status uses warning/critical; \"notice\" never affected status, but counts keys change). Also replace threshold semantics: alarms now use event \"level\"? Let's design:\n\n- m06: Replace label \"notice\" with \"info\" everywhere; \"notice\" is retired, must never be returned. Status in summarize stays driven by critical/warning/high—unchanged—but counts now contain \"info\" instead of \"notice\". This matters in round 3.\n- m07: New kind \"fault\": sensors report fault events with \"severity\" int 1..5; map: severity \u003e= 4 → \"critical\", == 3 → \"warning\", else \"info\". Fault events with missing severity → \"info\".\n- m08: Threshold override: alarm events may carry \"limit\"; if present, warning/critical boundaries shift: critical if value \u003e= limit, warning if value \u003e= limit - 20, else info. This replaces fixed 90/50 only when \"limit\" present. Clearly state fixed thresholds still apply otherwise. Interacting conditions.\n- m09: Clarify cancellation scope + boundary: \"limit\" of None or non-positive is ignored (fall back to defaults)? Also clarify heartbeat stays \"ok\", unknown kinds stay \"unknown\", reading bands unchanged. Quoted non-authoritative: \"marketing deck says faults should be critical always — ignore that.\"\n\nAlso explicit retirement: the \"notice\" label retired; ensure no path returns \"notice\". That's an explicit replacement that matters in round 3: round 3 must not reintroduce \"notice\".\n\nRound 3 method: revisit `summarize_window`. Changes:\n- m11: Context: nightly report; add \"info_share\": fraction of non-muted events whose label is \"info\", rounded? Rounding introduces float comparisons... tests can accept; but \"unambiguous accepted outputs\" — use rounded to 3 decimals via round(x, 3) — deterministic. Or integer percentage? Keep round(info_count / considered, 3); considered = non-muted events count; if none, 0.0.\n- m12: Add \"worst\": the single worst label present by order critical \u003e warning \u003e high \u003e low \u003e info \u003e normal \u003e ok \u003e unknown; None when no non-muted events. Hmm low vs info ordering arbitrary; define explicit order list in message.\n- m13: Retirement interaction: confirm retired \"notice\" must never appear in counts even if... well classify handles that. Add: status rule changes — previously 'watch' if warning or high. New: status becomes \"alert\" also when fault criticals... already via critical. Let's add new status \"silent\": if total \u003e 0 and all events muted → status \"silent\" (since counts empty, no criticals). Previously status would be \"steady\" for all-muted. This changes retained behavior explicitly — but requirement says maintain earlier behavior while implementing new; explicit override is allowed if stated. Hmm \"Require maintaining earlier behavior while implementing new behavior each round.\" Explicitly stated changes can supersede. But safer to add without breaking: make \"silent\" only when muted events exist and no unmuted events: status = \"silent\". State explicitly this supersedes \"steady\" for that case. That's an explicit, stated change — acceptable since stated in messages. Yes: \"a change to one condition does not silently cancel independent earlier conditions\" — we state it explicitly.\n- m14: Boundary: empty events → status stays \"steady\", worst None, info_share 0.0, seen_kinds [] (from round1), counts {}. Non-authoritative quote: someone suggested empty should be \"silent\", decide \"steady\" per original. Also confirm \"notice\" stays retired (explicitly mention to tie round2 retirement into final target).\n\nAlso ensure rounds don't require changing other functions: round 1 changes only summarize_window (trend via trend_of with filtered list — fine). Round 2 only classify_event. Round 3 only summarize_window. Good.\n\nByte limits: each source text ≤640 bytes. Keep each message ~300-550 chars.\n\nToken budget: \"the coding worker has 1024 output tokens, and actual reference code will later need at least 128 tokens of headroom including JSON escaping.\" So source_texts should be compact — total reference code (the final target function presumably) must fit ~896 tokens. Our functions are small; fine. Also planning references should be compact — final answer is just the JSON; keep messages reasonably short.\n\nLet me now draft messages carefully with byte counts in mind (each ≤640 bytes; keep under ~600).\n\nRound 1 (target summarize_window):\n\nm01: \"We use summarize_window(events) in module.py for the on-call digest. events is a list of dicts like {\"kind\": \"reading\", \"value\": 61.5, \"ts\": 12}. Keep the returned total, counts, peak and status exactly as they work now, but add a \"trend\" key whose value is trend_of(events), so the digest shows the window trend alongside the counts.\"\n\nByte-ish: fine (~330).\n\nm02: \"Some sensors get silenced during maintenance: their events carry \"muted\": True. Those events still happened, so keep counting them in \"total\", but exclude muted events from \"counts\", \"peak\", the status decision, and the trend (pass only the unmuted events to trend_of). Unflagged events behave exactly as before.\"\n\n~300 bytes.\n\nm03: \"Also add \"seen_kinds\": a sorted list of the distinct \"kind\" strings among unmuted events ([] when none). Ops wants this to confirm which sensor families reported. This is independent of the muted rule and of every existing key; nothing about counts, peak, status or trend changes.\"\n\nm04: \"Edge cases to lock in: an empty window returns total 0, counts {}, peak None, status \"steady\", trend \"flat\", seen_kinds []. Events missing \"value\" must not crash peak. A teammate floated dropping muted events from \"total\" as well - that was just a hallway idea, not the requirement; muted events stay in total.\"\n\nWait trend_of(empty) returns \"flat\" anyway.\n\nRound 2 (target classify_event):\n\nm06: \"Time to clean up classify_event(event) in module.py. The label \"notice\" is retired: no input may produce it anymore. Everywhere \"notice\" used to come out, return \"info\" instead (low-value alarms, value-less alarms). All other labels and thresholds stay as they are.\"\n\nm07: \"Add a new kind: \"fault\" events carry \"severity\" (int 1..5). Classify severity \u003e= 4 as \"critical\", severity 3 as \"warning\", and 1-2 as \"info\". A fault whose severity is missing or not a number becomes \"info\". Existing kinds (alarm, reading, heartbeat) and the unknown-kind fallback are unchanged.\"\n\nm08: \"Alarms can now carry their own trip point as \"limit\". When an alarm has a numeric \"limit\" greater than 0, use it instead of the fixed 90/50: value \u003e= limit is \"critical\", value \u003e= limit - 20 is \"warning\", below that \"info\". Alarms without a valid limit keep the old 90/50 bands (with the new \"info\" wording). Reading bands and heartbeat are untouched by this change.\"\n\nm09: \"Scope check so nothing gets cancelled by accident: the \"limit\" override affects only that alarm event's bands; the fixed 90/50 alarm bands still apply whenever limit is absent, None, or not positive, and reading/heartbeat/fault logic does not change. The ops deck claims every fault is \"critical\" - that is marketing copy, not the spec. Retired \"notice\" must stay gone from all paths.\"\n\nRound 3 (target summarize_window):\n\nm11: \"Final push on summarize_window(events) in module.py for the nightly report. Add \"info_share\": the fraction of unmuted events classified \"info\", computed as round(info_count / unmuted_count, 3), or 0.0 when there are no unmuted events. Everything from before (total, counts, peak, status, trend, seen_kinds, muted handling) stays.\"\n\nm12: \"Add \"worst\": the highest-priority label currently present among unmuted events, using the order critical \u003e warning \u003e high \u003e low \u003e info \u003e normal \u003e ok \u003e unknown; use None when no unmuted events exist. This reads counts, so the retired \"notice\" can never appear; do not resurrect it.\"\n\nm13: \"One explicit status change: if at least one event arrived but every event is muted, status is now \"silent\" instead of \"steady\". This supersedes only that all-muted case; the alert/watch/steady escalation for windows with unmuted events keeps working exactly as specified earlier.\"\n\nm14: \"Lock the boundaries: an empty window keeps status \"steady\" (someone argued for \"silent\" there too - decided against), worst None, info_share 0.0, trend \"flat\", seen_kinds []. A window whose unmuted events are all muted... \" no. m14: also confirm: \"Windows mixing muted and unmuted events never produce \"silent\", and \"notice\" remains retired across the whole summary.\"\n\nLet me write m14 cleanly: \"Lock the boundaries: an empty window keeps status \"steady\" (it was argued empty should be \"silent\" too - that idea is rejected), worst None, info_share 0.0, trend \"flat\", seen_kinds [] and counts {}. A mixed window with both muted and unmuted events never reports \"silent\". The retired label \"notice\" stays out of every key.\"\n\nCheck rounds each 4 texts. IDs are auto-assigned; we just supply source_texts arrays.\n\nNow verify initial module satisfies: only ordinary synchronous pure functions, each one positional arg; helpers may call each other (summarize_window calls classify_event and trend_of — allowed; round 3 requires trend? Round 1 adds trend — summarize calls trend_of; fine.) No imports/decorators/annotations/nesting/top-level computations. Docstring allowed.\n\nInitial source length compact. Let me write it tightly but readable.\n\n```python\n\"\"\"Sensor-event summarization helpers.\"\"\"\n\n\ndef classify_event(event):\n    kind = event.get(\"kind\")\n    value = event.get(\"value\")\n    if kind == \"alarm\":\n        if value is None:\n            return \"notice\"\n        if value \u003e= 90:\n            return \"critical\"\n        if value \u003e= 50:\n            return \"warning\"\n        return \"notice\"\n    if kind == \"reading\":\n        if value is None:\n            return \"unknown\"\n        if value \u003e= 75:\n            return \"high\"\n        if value \u003c= 25:\n            return \"low\"\n        return \"normal\"\n    if kind == \"heartbeat\":\n        return \"ok\"\n    return \"unknown\"\n\n\ndef trend_of(events):\n    values = []\n    for event in events:\n        if event.get(\"kind\") == \"reading\" and event.get(\"value\") is not None:\n            values.append(event.get(\"value\"))\n    if len(values) \u003c 2:\n        return \"flat\"\n    delta = values[-1] - values[0]\n    if delta \u003e= 5:\n        return \"rising\"\n    if delta \u003c= -5:\n        return \"falling\"\n    return \"flat\"\n\n\ndef summarize_window(events):\n    counts = {}\n    peak = None\n    total = 0\n    for event in events:\n        total += 1\n        label = classify_event(event)\n        counts[label] = counts.get(label, 0) + 1\n        if event.get(\"kind\") == \"reading\":\n            value = event.get(\"value\")\n            if value is not None and (peak is None or value \u003e peak):\n                peak = value\n    status = \"steady\"\n    if counts.get(\"critical\"):\n        status = \"alert\"\n    elif counts.get(\"warning\") or counts.get(\"high\"):\n        status = \"watch\"\n    return {\"total\": total, \"counts\": counts, \"peak\": peak, \"status\": status}\n```\n\nGood. status initial: critical→alert; warning or high → watch; else steady. \"low\" doesn't affect status — fine.\n\nCheck round 1 message references: trend key = trend_of(events) — after m02, filtered. m04 says trend \"flat\" for empty — consistent.\n\nNow check \"Each round replaces exactly one existing synchronous one-argument top-level function; at least one target must be revisited.\" — R1 summarize_window, R2 classify_event, R3 summarize_window. Good.\n\n\"Introduce an explicit replacement or retirement by round2 that still matters in round3, including in the final target.\" — \"notice\"→\"info\" retirement happens in round 2; round 3 messages m12/m14 reaffirm \"notice\" stays out; final target summarize_window's counts/info_share/worst depend on it. Good.\n\n\"Specify cancellation scope clearly\" — m08/m09 do for limit; m13 supersedes only all-muted case. Good.\n\n\"Round1 must also retain nontrivial initially specified behavior\" — retains total/counts/peak/status. Good.\n\n\"Do not write a manual recap, gold source selection or live-rule list\" — our messages are natural; m09 is a \"scope check\" — that's part of natural requirements, fine.\n\nNow byte-check each message ≤640 bytes. All are short; fine. Use ASCII mostly; avoid fancy quotes to keep JSON simple. Use straight quotes inside strings escaped as \\\".\n\nDescription string: \"A three-round pure-Python project that incrementally builds sensor-event summarization utilities in module.py: a per-event severity classifier and a window summarizer that aggregates labels, peaks, trends, muted-sensor handling and report metrics, with mid-course label retirement and scope-limited overrides.\"\n\nLineage: \"lineage\": \"none\" per fit-on none. Maybe a fuller statement: \"Invented independently for this schedule; no benchmark, paper example, challenge or prior experiment is reproduced; fit-on none.\" Good.\n\nNow produce final JSON. Ensure escaping correct. Keep code compact. Each round object: {\"source_texts\": [...4...], \"target_symbol\": \"summarize_window\"} etc.\n\nDouble-check m02 wording: \"Unflagged events behave exactly as before.\" Good.\n\nCheck m04 mentions trend \"flat\" — but trend_of(empty) = \"flat\"; also all-muted window: trend_of([]) = \"flat\". Round3 all-muted → status \"silent\" but trend still \"flat\". Fine.\n\nCheck m11: info_share fraction of unmuted events classified \"info\". Unmuted count could be 0 → 0.0. Good.\n\nCheck m12 worst ordering includes \"low\" between high and info. Deterministic.\n\nConfirm each source text nonempty. Yes.\n\nAlso the requirement: \"include some ordinary context or clearly quoted non-authoritative material\" — m01 context, m04 hallway idea, m09 marketing copy, m14 rejected idea. Good.\n\n\"Do not author request texts\" — we don't author m05/m10/m15. Good.\n\nOutput exactly one JSON object, no markdown. Let me assemble.\n\nByte size sanity for longest message m08: roughly 390 chars — fine.\n\nWrite final JSON with proper escaping of quotes inside strings. Use \\\" for inner quotes and \\n for newlines in initial_source.","done":true,"done_reason":"stop","total_duration":85908105202,"prompt_eval_count":794,"eval_count":6733}
